      "text": "## Seleskovitch's Interpretive Theory"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-INTERPRETATION.md#010",
      "path": [
        "07-INTERPRETATION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:translation:07-interpretation, git-history:translation:07-interpretation]",
        "canonical_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "current_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "id": "maxim:translation:interpretation",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "translation",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "section": "translation",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Simultaneous and Consecutive Interpretation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Danica Seleskovitch developed the dominant cognitive theory of interpretation at ESIT (Ecole Supérieure d'Interprètes et de Traducteurs), Paris, 1970s:"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-INTERPRETATION.md#011",
      "path": [
        "07-INTERPRETATION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:translation:07-interpretation, git-history:translation:07-interpretation]",
        "canonical_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "current_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "id": "maxim:translation:interpretation",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "translation",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "section": "translation",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Simultaneous and Consecutive Interpretation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Core claim**: Interpretation is not word-for-word or even phrase-for-phrase translation. It is a three-stage process:"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-INTERPRETATION.md#012",
      "path": [
        "07-INTERPRETATION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:translation:07-interpretation, git-history:translation:07-interpretation]",
        "canonical_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "current_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "id": "maxim:translation:interpretation",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "translation",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "section": "translation",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Simultaneous and Consecutive Interpretation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nSELESKOVITCH'S INTERPRETIVE THEORY — THREE STAGES\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-INTERPRETATION.md#013",
      "path": [
        "07-INTERPRETATION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:translation:07-interpretation, git-history:translation:07-interpretation]",
        "canonical_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "current_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "id": "maxim:translation:interpretation",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "translation",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "section": "translation",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Simultaneous and Consecutive Interpretation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "STAGE 1: COMPREHENSION\n  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ The interpreter listens and builds a meaning         │\n  │ representation. This is NOT a linguistic             │\n  │ representation — it is a concept/meaning             │\n  │ representation that has been \"deverbalized.\"         │\n  │                                                      │\n  │ The words drop away; the meaning remains.            │\n  │                                                      │\n  │ This is why you often cannot remember the exact      │\n  │ words of something you understood — you retained     │\n  │ the meaning and discarded the linguistic form.       │\n  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n            │\n            ▼\n  STAGE 2: DEVERBALIZATION\n  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ The meaning is held briefly as a non-linguistic      │\n  │ concept — the \"sense\" that Cicero and Nida           │\n  │ were talking about.                                  │\n  │                                                      │\n  │ This is the interpretive moment — the cognitive      │\n  │ gap where the interpreter holds meaning without      │\n  │ words.                                               │\n  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n            │\n            ▼\n  STAGE 3: RE-EXPRESSION\n  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ The meaning is re-expressed in the target language.  │\n  │ The interpreter chooses target-language words to     │\n  │ encode the deverbalized meaning.                     │\n  │                                                      │\n  │ The target-language words are NOT derived from       │\n  │ the source-language words directly — they are        │\n  │ derived from the meaning.                            │\n  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-INTERPRETATION.md#014",
      "path": [
        "07-INTERPRETATION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:translation:07-interpretation, git-history:translation:07-interpretation]",
        "canonical_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "current_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "id": "maxim:translation:interpretation",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "translation",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "section": "translation",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Simultaneous and Consecutive Interpretation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "WHY THIS MATTERS:\n  It explains why trained interpreters often produce more\n  natural target-language output than word-for-word\n  translators: they are NOT translating words, they\n  are expressing meanings."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-INTERPRETATION.md#015",
      "path": [
        "07-INTERPRETATION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:translation:07-interpretation, git-history:translation:07-interpretation]",
        "canonical_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "current_path": "translation/07-INTERPRETATION.md",
        "id": "maxim:translation:interpretation",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "translation",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[interpretation]",
        "section": "translation",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Simultaneous and Consecutive Interpretation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "It also explains a failure mode:\n  If the interpreter does not understand (no deverbalization),\n  they fall back on word-for-word — which produces\n  errors and unnatural target text.\n```"
    },
    {
